WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.072
User feedback: useful pointer, biased measurement.

2
00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:05.315
Users give you two kinds of signal.

3
00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:11.995
Explicit feedback is what they tell you: thumbs, star ratings, “report a problem,” free-text comments.

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:24.029
Implicit feedback is what they do: copy the answer, click a cited source, rephrase the same question, abandon the session, escalate to a human, accept or reject a suggested edit.

5
00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:35.893
Chip Huyen’s AI Engineering (chapter 10) treats feedback design as an engineering concern for exactly this reason: it is one of the few signals that comes from real use at scale.

6
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.243
Each kind is biased in its own way.

7
00:00:38.523 --> 00:00:45.776
Explicit feedback is self-selected: a few percent of users rate anything, and they skew toward the delighted and the annoyed.

8
00:00:46.056 --> 00:00:51.987
Anthropic’s agent-evals guide calls user feedback sparse and skewed toward severe problems.

9
00:00:52.267 --> 00:01:05.451
Ratings also reward style: the Chatbot Arena team’s own analysis found that answer length was the dominant style factor in human votes, and a reader often can’t tell a confident wrong answer from a right one.

10
00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:12.173
(LLM judges share related biases, as Zheng and colleagues documented in 2023.)

11
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:21.755
Implicit signals are ambiguous: a user who doesn’t rephrase may be satisfied or may have given up; a copied answer may be copied into a complaint.

12
00:01:22.035 --> 00:01:39.613
And they carry position and presentation bias: in a classic eye-tracking study of web search, Joachims and colleagues (2005) found people clicked top-ranked results more even when the ranking was deliberately made worse, so clicks partly measure position and trust, not relevance.

13
00:01:39.893 --> 00:01:42.731
Your cited-source click rate has the same problem.

14
00:01:43.011 --> 00:01:53.251
There is also a feedback loop hazard: if you tune toward what users rate highly, you can amplify the bias (more confident, more agreeable answers) instead of quality.

15
00:01:53.531 --> 00:01:55.515
So use feedback for what it is good at.

16
00:01:55.795 --> 00:02:00.936
Thumbs-down and escalations are excellent pointers: every one goes into the review queue.

17
00:02:01.216 --> 00:02:05.760
Rephrase and abandonment rates are useful trend alarms when they move suddenly.

18
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:12.803
But the quality number you report comes from expert review of a random sample, and the judge you calibrated against it.
